-- EXPECT-LINES: 15 29 31 33 35 37
-- EXPECT-SOUND: 2
-- FIVE damaged equations in a row: every one is reported.
--
-- This was the case that proved the old anchor rule too narrow. Recovery
-- resynchronised only at a declaration keyword or `name :`, so a damaged
-- EQUATION was not an anchor and the skip walked over every remaining one --
-- five faults collapsed into one report, and the batch stopped early exactly
-- when a file was uniformly broken, which is when a work list helps most.
--
-- E4: resync_to_boundary already stops at a NEWLINE at the block's own level,
-- and the layout filter emits one exactly where an item begins, so the
-- boundary was always exact. The anchor's only remaining job is rejecting
-- wreckage, which is precisely the continuation-lead set.
module M`: all five damaged equations collapse into one error node
-- FIVE damaged equations in a row, and only the FIRST is reported.
--
-- This pins a real limitation rather than desired behaviour. Recovery in a
-- declaration block resynchronises only at an ANCHOR -- a declaration keyword,
-- or `name :` at the block column (D23 makes a signature the strongest anchor
-- available). A damaged EQUATION is not an anchor, so once the first fault
-- skips forward it walks over every remaining one to end of file.
--
-- Contrast 01, where sound declarations sit between the faults and act as
-- anchors: there all three are reported. So the batch stops early exactly
-- when a file is uniformly broken, which is when a work list would help most.
module M

a1 x := 1

a2 y := 2

a3 z := 3

a4 w := 4

a5 v := 5
